// D0166 — PROPOSED. What the human SAYS becomes a tracked item, and everything derived from it points
// back. Touches frozen schema/core (invariant 5), so it needs explicit sign-off and is NOT applied.
// #ProspectiveChange (D0070): adds one item type and one coverage view.
package Decision0166 {
    private import EngineElement::*;
    private import EngineWork::*;
    private import EngineVerification::*;
    private import EngineRelationships::*;

    #ProspectiveChange part d0166 : Decision {
        :>> id = "8c15b7a0-4d62-4e39-9f74-2a80ce163bd5";
        :>> title = "D0166: a human statement is a tracked item, and every Need or Story derived from one cites it";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-08-20";
        :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5";
        :>> status = DecisionStatus::proposed;
        :>> context = "THE HUMAN ASKED WHETHER PARSING THEIR STATEMENTS INTO USER STORIES AND TRACKING THEM IS ALREADY IN THE SCHEMA. PARTLY, AND THE MISSING HALF IS THE IMPORTANT ONE. What exists: a business layer with `Need` (a native requirement def carrying `source`, a solution-free `statement`, `marketJustification` and MoSCoW `priority`), `Persona` (description, goals) and `UseCase` (description, mainSuccess, extensions). What does NOT exist: any item holding what a human ACTUALLY SAID, with provenance. `Need.statement` is a REFINED, solution-free outcome authored by someone; it is not the utterance. And keel's `Story` is a delivery work item carrying estimatedPoints, actualPoints and actualHours - it has no narrative fields at all, so it is not a user story in the agile sense and the name collision is worth stating plainly. THIS SESSION IS THE EVIDENCE THAT THE GAP MATTERS. D0164 exists because a requirement was buried in an ACCEPTANCE NOTE ('viewpoints should be activateable/disableable just like processes'). issue137's record was corrected by a note in a review deck ('viewer on a single machine is still 1'). D0161 was redesigned three times by one-line questions. Every one of those arrived as a human statement, and every one was captured by ME copying it by hand into a procedureText - which means the model holds my transcription and the conversation holds the original. D0018 says that if resuming requires knowledge it belongs in the model, and the thing most often needed on resume is what they asked for.";
        :>> decision = "PROPOSED: one new item type and one computed view. THE TYPE, `Statement :> Element`, holds ONLY the utterance and its provenance - `text` (verbatim, never paraphrased), `saidBy` (an Actor), `saidAt`, and `channel` (where it was said: a console note, a deck note, a chat turn). IT CARRIES NO INTERPRETATION, which is the whole design: the reading of a statement is a SEPARATE authored item - a Need, a Story, an Issue, a Decision - linked back by a `#DerivedFrom` edge. THE INVARIANT THAT MAKES IT WORTH HAVING: a Statement is NEVER edited to agree with what was derived from it. If I misread them, the correction is a NEW derived item citing the same Statement, and the misreading stays visible as a superseded interpretation. That is D0108 ownership and §4 in one rule - their words are theirs, my reading is mine, and the two are separately auditable. THE VIEW, `keel statements`, reports two numbers that do not exist today: UNPARSED - statements with no derived item, meaning direction they gave that nothing acts on; and UNSOURCED - Needs, Stories and Decisions with no `#DerivedFrom` to any Statement, meaning work I invented rather than work they asked for. WHAT THIS IS NOT: it is not a transcript. Only statements that carry DIRECTION get recorded - a request, a correction, a constraint, a priority - because a log of everything said is a second corpus with no questions attached, which is exactly the mistake draft 1 of D0161 made.";
        :>> rationale = "WHY THE UTTERANCE AND THE READING MUST BE SEPARATE ITEMS: this session records me over-reading a two-word phrase into a confirmation ('confirmed otherwise'), and separately reading three field reports about a DESTINATION as reports about a CLICK. In both cases the human's words were exactly right and my interpretation was wrong, and in both cases the only record was my interpretation. If the utterance had been a tracked item with the reading pointing at it, the disagreement would have been visible in the model instead of surfacing three turns later. WHY `UNSOURCED` IS THE NUMBER I EXPECT TO BE UNCOMFORTABLE, and the reason to build this rather than the reason not to: most of the 354 sprints in this repository were charted by me, not requested. Some of that is correct - they hired an engine to run the process - but nobody can currently tell the ratio, and a project where the AI's inventions outnumber the human's requests by an unknown factor cannot answer 'is this building what was asked for'. That is a traceability question and this engine already answers the equivalent question for requirements (`keel rootedness`). WHY NOT REUSE `Need`: a Need is solution-free, refined and prioritised - it is already an interpretation, authored deliberately. Folding the raw utterance into it would destroy the distinction that makes the pair useful, and would put words in their mouth in the field named `statement`. WHY NOT A NEW WORD FOR user story: keel's `Story` is load-bearing across 354 sprint records and renaming it is a migration with no benefit. If a narrative form is wanted, it is `asA`/`iWant`/`soThat` on `Need` where the persona already lives - not a fourth work-item type.";
        :>> consequences = "IF ACCEPTED: `schema/core` gains one `part def` - which is a FROZEN-SCHEMA change (invariant 5) and the reason this is proposed rather than applied - plus one computed view, one marker in the edge vocabulary if `#DerivedFrom` needs widening, and a doc-sync pass. The recording cost is one item per directive statement, which at this session's rate is a handful per sitting; the read cost is zero, because it is all computed. THE HONEST LIMIT: nothing can force a statement to be recorded. A guard can report Needs and Decisions with no `#DerivedFrom`, which makes the OMISSION visible, but it cannot know about a statement nobody wrote down - so `unparsed` is a floor, never a total, and the view must say so rather than implying completeness. REMOVAL PATH, per D0163: delete the recorded Statements and the view; nothing else reads them, the `#DerivedFrom` edges become ordinary dangling-free edges to nothing only if the targets are deleted too, so remove edges with their statements. Needs, Stories and Decisions are unaffected - they were never dependent on having a source, which is also why this degrades gracefully if adoption lapses. IF REJECTED: their statements stay in conversation history, my transcriptions stay in procedureText fields, and the question 'how much of this backlog did anyone ask for' stays unanswerable - which is survivable, and worth being a recorded position rather than an accident.";
    }
}
